"# Chapter 12 — Apropos and search: introspecting by shape

You know what you want. You do not remember what the verb is called. Search bridges the gap.

Motoi's search family runs over the registry (Chapter 11) with different match criteria: by name (`apropos`), by arity, by contract shape, by doc-string content. Each is one filter over the registry. Each returns a list of verb names.

## 12.1 apropos — the workhorse

`apropos` takes a regex or substring and returns every verb whose name matches.

```scheme
;; apropos is not a primitive — it IS the chapter's thesis, made concrete:
;; a substring filter over (all-verbs). Define it once and reuse it.
(define (apropos s)
  (filter (lambda (n) (string-contains n s)) (all-verbs)))

;; meta-field pulls one keyword-keyed field out of (help name), null-safe.
(define (meta-field key n)
  (let ((m (help n)))
    (and m (let ((kv (assq key m))) (and kv (cadr kv))))))

;; The registry has ~1700 verbs; calling (help n) on all of them is expensive.
;; In the book we demonstrate the *shape* of each search over a bounded sample.
;; To search the whole registry, swap (registry-sample) for (all-verbs).
(define registry-sample (take (all-verbs) 60))

(apropos \"sort\")
;; => (\"sort\")

(apropos \"cortex\")
;; => (\"artifact/subscribe-cortex\" \"cortex/associate\" \"cortex/forget\"
;;     \"cortex/keys\" \"cortex/lookup\" \"cortex/prune\" \"cortex/query\"
;;     \"cortex/read\" \"cortex/recall\" \"cortex/record\" \"cortex/remember\"
;;     \"cortex/search\" \"cortex/size\" \"cortex/topk\" \"cortex/write\")

(apropos \"paint-\")
;; => (\"paint-cell\" \"paint-clear\" \"paint-text\")
```

Three searches, three lessons.

`\"sort\"` returns exactly one name. Motoi has a single sorting verb — `sort` — and no `sort!`, no `sort-by`, no `merge-sort`. That is a real answer, and a useful one: a one-element result tells you there is nothing else to look for, so stop guessing at names and go read `(doc-of 'sort)`.

`\"cortex\"` returns fifteen, and one of them — `artifact/subscribe-cortex` — matched in the *middle* of its name. `apropos` is a substring search, not a namespace filter. That is usually what you want when you are hunting; use `verbs-in-namespace` (below) when you want the family and nothing else.

`\"paint-\"` returns three. The trailing hyphen narrowed it: without it, `\"paint\"` would also pull in `flower/paint`.

Note the results are **strings**, not symbols, because that is what `all-verbs` returns.

At the REPL: `,apropos <substring>` prints the matching names one per line, sorted. Motoi's implementation lives in `src/repl.js`.

## 12.2 verbs-in-namespace

`(verbs-in-namespace ns)` returns every verb whose name is prefixed with `ns/`. This was shown in Chapter 11.

```scheme
;; verbs-in-namespace = apropos anchored to a "ns/" prefix.
(define (verbs-in-namespace ns)
  (let ((prefix (string-append ns \"/\")))
    (filter (lambda (n) (string-prefix? prefix n)) (all-verbs))))

(verbs-in-namespace \"composer\")
;; => (\"composer/adsr\" \"composer/apply\" \"composer/button\" \"composer/canvas\"
;;     \"composer/cart-apply\" ... \"composer/voice-pool\")   ;; 26 in all
```

Namespace scope for search when you know the family.

## 12.3 doc-search

Search doc strings (not just names) for a keyword:

```scheme
;; scans docs across the sample; swap registry-sample for (all-verbs) for all
(define (doc-search keyword)
  (filter
    (lambda (n)
      (let ((d (doc-of n)))
        (and d (string-contains d keyword))))
    registry-sample))    ;; swap for (all-verbs) to search everything

(doc-search \"predicate\")
;; => every verb whose doc mentions the word predicate
```

Doc-search finds verbs whose name does not include the concept but whose behavior does. Chapter 11's grep-docs was the same pattern.

## 12.4 find-by-arity — search by shape

The registry knows every verb's arity. Combine `all-verbs` and `arity-of` and you have a shape-search.

```scheme
(define (find-by-arity n)
  (filter (lambda (v)
            (let ((a (arity-of v)))
              (and (number? a) (= a n))))
          registry-sample))    ;; swap for (all-verbs) to search everything

(find-by-arity 2)
;; => every fixed-arity-2 verb — the binary-operator set.
```

When you're writing a higher-order helper that expects a two-arg function, `(find-by-arity 2)` tells you every verb that qualifies.

## 12.5 find-by-contract shape

Contracts are strings. Substring search is enough:

```scheme
(define (find-by-contract fragment)
  (filter (lambda (v)
            (let ((c (type-of v)))
              (and c (string-contains c fragment))))
          registry-sample))    ;; swap for (all-verbs) to search everything

(find-by-contract "-> boolean")   ;; the predicate set
(find-by-contract "list")         ;; the list-processing set
```

Text-level shape search — not typed, but useful.

## 12.6 find-by-perm — the audit set

Every verb carries a `:perm` field. Filter by it:

```scheme
(define (find-by-perm p)
  (filter (lambda (v)
            (equal? (meta-field :perm v) p))
          registry-sample))    ;; swap for (all-verbs) to search everything

(find-by-perm \"state-change\")
;; => every verb that writes state. Audit target.
```

A cart that calls only `'read` verbs is inherently safe. A cart that reaches for `'state-change` verbs needs closer scrutiny. This search names the audit set.

## 12.7 find-by-source-file

Provenance search:

```scheme
(define (verbs-from file)
  (filter (lambda (v)
            (let ((s (source-of v)))
              (and s (string-contains s file))))
          registry-sample))    ;; swap for (all-verbs) to search everything

(verbs-from "audio.js")
```

This is how the book generator finds which chapter to describe each verb in. It's how the IDE tooltip finds where to jump. It's how deprecation sweeps find affected verbs.

## 12.8 did-you-mean — the small-model reflex

When a user or the model types a name that doesn't exist, the runtime tries to guess. The mechanism is edit-distance search across `(all-verbs)`, ranked by closeness.

```scheme
;; Edit distance is pure Scheme — define it, don't wish for a primitive.
(define (levenshtein a b)
  (let ((la (string-length a)) (lb (string-length b)))
    (let ((prev (make-vector (+ lb 1) 0)))
      (do ((j 0 (+ j 1))) ((> j lb)) (vector-set! prev j j))
      (do ((i 1 (+ i 1))) ((> i la) (vector-ref prev lb))
        (let ((cur (make-vector (+ lb 1) 0)))
          (vector-set! cur 0 i)
          (do ((j 1 (+ j 1))) ((> j lb))
            (let ((cost (if (char=? (string-ref a (- i 1))
                                    (string-ref b (- j 1))) 0 1)))
              (vector-set! cur j
                (min (+ (vector-ref prev j) 1)
                     (+ (vector-ref cur (- j 1)) 1)
                     (+ (vector-ref prev (- j 1)) cost)))))
          (do ((j 0 (+ j 1))) ((> j lb)) (vector-set! prev j (vector-ref cur j))))))))

;; Searching the whole registry with edit distance twice is expensive; in the
;; book we search a bounded sample so the example stays cheap. The mechanism is
;; identical — swap (verb-sample) for (all-verbs) to search everything.
(define verb-sample (take (all-verbs) 25))

(define (did-you-mean name)
  (let* ((n (string-length name))
         (candidates (filter (lambda (v)
                               (let ((d (levenshtein name v)))
                                 (< d (max 2 (quotient n 3)))))
                             verb-sample)))
    (sort candidates
          (lambda (a b)
            (< (levenshtein name a)
               (levenshtein name b))))))

(did-you-mean \"csr\")
;; => (\"cdr\" ...) — closest edits first.
```

Chapter 13 extends this with a model-augmented version — when edit distance runs out, the model can suggest semantically similar verbs. The base version is pure and fast.

## 12.9 Combining searches — the composition dividend

Each search returns a list. Lists compose:

```scheme
;; intersection of N lists = fold with a pairwise keep-if-in-both.
(define (intersection2 xs ys)
  (filter (lambda (x) (member x ys)) xs))
(define (intersection . lists)
  (if (null? lists)
      '()
      (fold intersection2 (car lists) (cdr lists))))

;; Every binary state-changing verb whose impl is in the audio module.
;; (verbs-from is defined in 12.7, below.)
(define (verbs-from file)
  (filter (lambda (v)
            (let ((s (source-of v)))
              (and s (string-contains s file))))
          registry-sample))    ;; swap for (all-verbs) to search everything
(intersection (find-by-arity 2)
              (find-by-perm \"state-change\")
              (verbs-from \"audio.js\"))
```

Highly specific query. Emerges from three primitive searches. Neither the primitives nor the composition were added — both were already there.

## 12.10 Ranked search results

Two common rankings.

**By call frequency** — hot verbs first.

```scheme
(define (search-ranked query)
  (sort (apropos query)
        (lambda (a b) (> (stats/verb-calls-of a)
                         (stats/verb-calls-of b)))))
```

**By doc completeness** — documented first.

```scheme
(define (search-documented-first query)
  (sort (apropos query)
        (lambda (a b) (and (doc-of a) (not (doc-of b))))))
```

Both compose the stats counter with the search. Both are three lines. Both would be full features in other languages; here they're compositions.

## 12.11 Search at the REPL — the meta-command

At the REPL, `,apropos` is the shortcut:

```
> ,apropos map
map · filter-map · for-each · map-indexed · flat-map ...
```

The command calls the underlying `apropos` verb but formats the result for interactive reading — one verb per line, docstring snippet on the right if space allows. Namespace patterns work too:

```
> ,apropos audio/
audio/play · audio/halt · audio/tempo · audio/bar-clock ...
```

## 12.12 Custom search predicates — the general shape

Every search is one shape:

```
(filter <predicate> (all-verbs))
```

The predicate is what changes. That's why the language doesn't ship a hundred `find-by-X` verbs — you write the predicate you need.

Example: find every verb whose docstring mentions "cortex".

```scheme
(filter (lambda (v)
          (let ((d (doc-of v)))
            (and d (string-contains d "cortex"))))
        registry-sample)    ;; swap for (all-verbs) to search everything
```

Twelve tokens including whitespace. That's the payoff of composable primitives.

## 12.13 Search across dialects — Motoi vs the sister dialect

The sister dialect is Motoi's downstream variant. Its verb set overlaps significantly but not completely.

```scheme
;; With only one dialect loaded we can only ask the local registry. Model the
;; sister dialect's verb set as any list of names; membership is then computable.
(define (verb-in-registry? v names)
  (and (member v names) #t))

(define (in-both? v sister-names)
  (and (verb-in-registry? v (all-verbs))   ;; in Motoi
       (verb-in-registry? v sister-names))) ;; in the sister dialect

(in-both? \"map\" (list \"map\" \"filter\"))
```

When the registries drift, the discrepancy is a computable value, not a verbal mystery.

## 12.14 Search + Cortex — personalized vocabulary

Cortex holds every recorded event. One thing worth searching: which verbs has this user actually called?

```scheme
(define (verbs-user-has-called)
  (let ((events (cortex/recall 'verb-call-events)))
    (if (pair? events)
        (delete-duplicates
          (map (lambda (e) (cdr (assoc :verb e))) events))
        '())))

(verbs-user-has-called)   ;; => () until events are recorded
```

That's a personalized vocabulary — the subset of the language THIS person uses. The doc-priority tool from chapter 11 gets sharper when weighted by personal usage.

## 12.15 Search as a training signal

Every `apropos` call is a labeled example. The query is the intent; the returned verbs are the candidates; the one the user actually calls next is the ground-truth label. Record those triples in Cortex and you eventually train a better `apropos` — one that ranks by observed selection, not just string match.

## 12.16 Search as a shape query — closing

Search in Motoi is not a special mechanism. It's the composition of `all-verbs` with a predicate. Every search you can imagine is just a different predicate over the same list.

That means when you're writing a new tool that needs to find verbs by some criterion, you're not extending the language — you're choosing which alist field to filter on. `arity-of`, `type-of`, `perm-of`, `source-of`, `doc-of`, `stats/verb-calls-of` — six axes, and you can filter by any of them, or by any combination.

The language exposes its own shape. You compose queries against that shape. No new primitive was ever required. This is what "the language answers questions about itself" actually means in practice.

## 12.17 The reflex

When you know what you want but not the name:

1. `(apropos \"keyword\")` — try the name first
2. `(doc-search \"keyword\")` — try the docs if the name search was thin
3. `(verbs-in-namespace \"ns\")` — try the namespace if you know the family
4. `(did-you-mean 'guess)` — LLM-augmented for tricky cases (Chapter 13)

Four options. Try them in order. One of them will hit.

## 12.18 A worked example

You want to compute standard deviation. You do not remember what Motoi calls it.

```scheme
(apropos \"dev\")
;; => (deviate std-dev standard-deviation mean-abs-dev)
```

Three candidates. Which one?

```scheme
(describe 'std-dev)
;; std-dev [math] — arity 1
;; Return the standard deviation of a list of numbers.
```

Perfect. Copy from `(examples-of 'std-dev)`, adapt, done.

Two searches, one describe, one examples. From *I need standard deviation* to *I have a working call* in four keystrokes.

